		<Placemark id="4c5ae659_KFPCTKJEXBCI12809582A9841B2C">
			<name>F9-024 JCSat-14 ASDS position</name>
			<description><![CDATA[<div><b>name</b>: F9-024 JCSat-14 ASDS position</div><div><b>description</b>: Landing - 06-May-16 - Falcon 9 v1.2. Flight 24, booster B1022 - Marmac304 OCISLY, Elsbeth III tug, Go Quest support

First GTO mission successful Falon 9 landing.
B1022 utilized as flown benchmark vehicle - done 8x Mcgregor full static fire tests.

661km downrange
Reentry speed 1750 m/s. Landing burn at 850 m altitude. Three-engines landing burn, followed by central engine final landing. Touchdown at 1.1 m/s, 2 m from bulls eye. Three seconds of propellant remaining.

@elonmusk 6 May 2016:
Yeah, this was a three engine landing burn, so triple deceleration of last flight. That's important to minimize gravity losses.
@elonmusk 16 May 2016:
Most recent rocket took max damage, due to v high entry velocity. Will be our life leader for ground tests to confirm others are good.
@elonmusk 22 May 2016:
Flight 24 is def capable of flying again, but it makes sense to apply ground delta qual to rocket w toughest entry conditions.

FCC APPLICATION: https://goo.gl/bHd44k
</div>]]></description>
