		<Placemark id="4c5ae659_14EC08937414A9E0">
			<name>F9-029 Amos-6 planned launch groundtrack</name>
			<description><![CDATA[<div><b>name</b>: F9-029 Amos-6 planned launch groundtrack</div><div><b>description</b>: Middle track inside hazard areas https://goo.gl/VQKBXz

Planned Launch - 03-Sep-16 - Falcon 9 v1.2. - CCAFS SLC-40

~5250 kg GEO satellite (slot 4°W)

01-Sep-16 - Loss of the vehicle and its payload about T-8min in advance of a static fire test at SLC-40.
One of the three COPVs inside 2nd stage LOX tank failed - accumulation of LOX in a buckle between the COPV liner and overwrap, subsequent creating solid SOX by cold helium, which becoming trapped when COPV pressurized, leading to friction ignition and COPV failure. 

@elonmusk 01 Sep 2016:
Loss of Falcon vehicle today during propellant fill operation. Originated around upper stage oxygen tank. Cause still unknown. More soon.
@elonmusk 09 Sep 2016:
Still working on the Falcon fireball investigation. Turning out to be the most difficult and complex failure we have ever had in 14 years.
Important to note that this happened during a routine filling operation. Engines were not on and there was no apparent heat source.
Particularly trying to understand the quieter bang sound a few seconds before the fireball goes off. May come from rocket or something else.
</div>]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#__managed_style_02207D65342C6E063455</styleUrl>
			<ExtendedData>
				<SchemaData schemaUrl="#__managed_schema">
					<SimpleData name="str:bmFtZQ==">F9-029 Amos-6 planned launch groundtrack</SimpleData>
					<SimpleData name="str:ZGVzY3JpcHRpb24=">Middle track inside hazard areas https://goo.gl/VQKBXz

Planned Launch - 03-Sep-16 - Falcon 9 v1.2. - CCAFS SLC-40

~5250 kg GEO satellite (slot 4°W)

01-Sep-16 - Loss of the vehicle and its payload about T-8min in advance of a static fire test at SLC-40.
One of the three COPVs inside 2nd stage LOX tank failed - accumulation of LOX in a buckle between the COPV liner and overwrap, subsequent creating solid SOX by cold helium, which becoming trapped when COPV pressurized, leading to friction ignition and COPV failure. 

@elonmusk 01 Sep 2016:
Loss of Falcon vehicle today during propellant fill operation. Originated around upper stage oxygen tank. Cause still unknown. More soon.
@elonmusk 09 Sep 2016:
Still working on the Falcon fireball investigation. Turning out to be the most difficult and complex failure we have ever had in 14 years.
Important to note that this happened during a routine filling operation. Engines were not on and there was no apparent heat source.
Particularly trying to understand the quieter bang sound a few seconds before the fireball goes off. May come from rocket or something else.
</SimpleData>
				</SchemaData>
			</ExtendedData>
