		<Placemark id="4c5ae659_1ECB95CEA03F1E2B">
			<name>M1390 Zuma launch groundtrack
</name>
			<description><![CDATA[<div><b>name</b>: M1390 Zuma launch groundtrack
</div><div><b>description</b>: Estimated track inside hazard areas https://goo.gl/L9HXtj

Launch - 08-Jan-18 - Falcon 9 v1.2.b4 Flight 47 - CCAFS SLC-40

Booster 1043, AL type of grid fins, Fairing 1.0.

Secretive payload - code-named Zuma contracted by government through Northrop Grumman for U.S. government.
Postponed from November due to fairing issue.
Estimated deployment into 900-1000km circular/50° orbit

Uncorfirmed rumours about payload demising together with S2 stage after separation failure of Payload Attach Fitting (PAF) provided by Northrop Grumman (not by SpaceX).
Spacex: “We do not comment on missions of this nature; but as of right now reviews of the data indicate Falcon 9 performed nominally.”

Planned S2 deorbit was confirmed by fuel dump observation over the Khartoum in Sudan after ~1.5 orbits.
Washed-up fairing debris found 12-Jan-18 at North Myrtle Beach, Horry County, SC
</div>]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#__managed_style_02207D65342C6E063455</styleUrl>
			<ExtendedData>
				<SchemaData schemaUrl="#__managed_schema">
					<SimpleData name="str:bmFtZQ==">M1390 Zuma launch groundtrack
</SimpleData>
					<SimpleData name="str:ZGVzY3JpcHRpb24=">Estimated track inside hazard areas https://goo.gl/L9HXtj

Launch - 08-Jan-18 - Falcon 9 v1.2.b4 Flight 47 - CCAFS SLC-40

Booster 1043, AL type of grid fins, Fairing 1.0.

Secretive payload - code-named Zuma contracted by government through Northrop Grumman for U.S. government.
Postponed from November due to fairing issue.
Estimated deployment into 900-1000km circular/50° orbit

Uncorfirmed rumours about payload demising together with S2 stage after separation failure of Payload Attach Fitting (PAF) provided by Northrop Grumman (not by SpaceX).
Spacex: “We do not comment on missions of this nature; but as of right now reviews of the data indicate Falcon 9 performed nominally.”

Planned S2 deorbit was confirmed by fuel dump observation over the Khartoum in Sudan after ~1.5 orbits.
Washed-up fairing debris found 12-Jan-18 at North Myrtle Beach, Horry County, SC
</SimpleData>
				</SchemaData>
			</ExtendedData>
