		<Placemark id="4c5ae659_207E72AB8A79F7A6">
			<name>LZ Dragon Static Fire Test Stand</name>
			<description><![CDATA[<div><b>name</b>: LZ Dragon Static Fire Test Stand</div><div><b>description</b>: 13 Nov 2019 - Crew Dragon C205 Static Fire Test

20 Apr 2019 - Crew Dragon C204.2 spacecraft suffers an anomaly during static fire testing

@JimBridenstine Apr 20 2019
The NASA and SpaceX teams are assessing the anomaly that occurred today during a part of the Dragon Super Draco Static Fire Test at SpaceX Landing Zone 1 in Florida. This is why we test. We will learn, make the necessary adjustments and safely move forward with our Commercial Crew Program.

Patricia Sanders Apr 25 2019
The event occurred during a static fire test conducted prior to the in-flight abort test. The firing was intended to demonstrate integrated systems SuperDraco performance in two times vehicle level vibro-acoustic-like for abort environments. Firing of 12 service section Dracos were successfully performed. Firing of eight SuperDracos resulted in an anomaly.

Hans Koenigsmann at CRS-17 Pre-Launch Presser 02 May 2019
Just before we wanted to fire the SuperDraco, there was an anomaly and the vehicle was destroyed.
Initial data indicates that the anomaly occurred during the activation of the SuperDraco system.
We have no reason to believe that there's an issue with the SuperDracos themselves.

SpaceX press release July 15, 2019
Initial data reviews indicated that the anomaly occurred approximately 100 milliseconds prior to ignition of Crew Dragon’s eight SuperDraco thrusters and during pressurization of the vehicle’s propulsion systems. Evidence shows that a leaking component allowed liquid oxidizer – nitrogen tetroxide (NTO) – to enter high-pressure helium tubes during ground processing. A slug of this NTO was driven through a helium check valve at high speed during rapid initialization of the launch escape system, resulting in structural failure within the check valve. The failure of the titanium component in a high-pressure NTO environment was sufficient to cause ignition of the check valve and led to an explosion.
</div>]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#__managed_style_68907E8E662C6E0634C2</styleUrl>
			<ExtendedData>
				<SchemaData schemaUrl="#__managed_schema">
					<SimpleData name="str:bmFtZQ==">LZ Dragon Static Fire Test Stand</SimpleData>
					<SimpleData name="str:ZGVzY3JpcHRpb24=">13 Nov 2019 - Crew Dragon C205 Static Fire Test

20 Apr 2019 - Crew Dragon C204.2 spacecraft suffers an anomaly during static fire testing

@JimBridenstine Apr 20 2019
The NASA and SpaceX teams are assessing the anomaly that occurred today during a part of the Dragon Super Draco Static Fire Test at SpaceX Landing Zone 1 in Florida. This is why we test. We will learn, make the necessary adjustments and safely move forward with our Commercial Crew Program.

Patricia Sanders Apr 25 2019
The event occurred during a static fire test conducted prior to the in-flight abort test. The firing was intended to demonstrate integrated systems SuperDraco performance in two times vehicle level vibro-acoustic-like for abort environments. Firing of 12 service section Dracos were successfully performed. Firing of eight SuperDracos resulted in an anomaly.

Hans Koenigsmann at CRS-17 Pre-Launch Presser 02 May 2019
Just before we wanted to fire the SuperDraco, there was an anomaly and the vehicle was destroyed.
Initial data indicates that the anomaly occurred during the activation of the SuperDraco system.
We have no reason to believe that there&apos;s an issue with the SuperDracos themselves.

SpaceX press release July 15, 2019
Initial data reviews indicated that the anomaly occurred approximately 100 milliseconds prior to ignition of Crew Dragon’s eight SuperDraco thrusters and during pressurization of the vehicle’s propulsion systems. Evidence shows that a leaking component allowed liquid oxidizer – nitrogen tetroxide (NTO) – to enter high-pressure helium tubes during ground processing. A slug of this NTO was driven through a helium check valve at high speed during rapid initialization of the launch escape system, resulting in structural failure within the check valve. The failure of the titanium component in a high-pressure NTO environment was sufficient to cause ignition of the check valve and led to an explosion.
</SimpleData>
				</SchemaData>
			</ExtendedData>
