		<Placemark id="4c5ae659_28C9090850172815">
			<name>Dragon Decontamination Building
</name>
			<description><![CDATA[<div><b>name</b>: Dragon Decontamination Building
</div><div><b>description</b>: Since the Dragon is a reusable spacecraft, it is recovered after flight. The vehicle is brought to
the McGregor testing facility where the remaining propellant is off-loaded into cylinders at the Dragon Decontamination Building. During propellant off-loading the propellant tanks that are onboard the Dragon Capsule are pressurized to push the propellant out and into liquid cylinders. The contaminated gas is then vented from the propellant tanks through the thermal oxidizer to the atrnosphere.
Once the propellant is off-loaded, decontamination of the capsule occurs. Decontamination of the Dragon spacecraft will also occur in the Dragon Decontamination Building. It is estimated that up to eight times per year, Spacex will need to decontaminate a Dragon Capsule. During this operation, there is a small risk of propellant being released to the atnosphere due to the small amount of liquid and vapor monomethylhydrazine (MMH) and nitrogen tetroxide (NTO) that remain in the unit after use. SpaceX is proposing to vent the residual MMH and NTO to the existing thermal oxidizer during thg decontamination activities.

https://records.tceq.texas.gov/cs/idcplg?IdcService=TCEQ_EXTERNAL_SEARCH_GET_FILE&dID=1393118&Rendition=Web

https://records.tceq.texas.gov/cs/idcplg?IdcService=TCEQ_EXTERNAL_SEARCH_GET_FILE&dID=1393128&Rendition=Web</div>]]></description>
			<styleUrl>#__managed_style_01B55663D42C6E0634F4</styleUrl>
			<ExtendedData>
				<SchemaData schemaUrl="#__managed_schema">
					<SimpleData name="str:bmFtZQ==">Dragon Decontamination Building
</SimpleData>
					<SimpleData name="str:ZGVzY3JpcHRpb24=">Since the Dragon is a reusable spacecraft, it is recovered after flight. The vehicle is brought to
the McGregor testing facility where the remaining propellant is off-loaded into cylinders at the Dragon Decontamination Building. During propellant off-loading the propellant tanks that are onboard the Dragon Capsule are pressurized to push the propellant out and into liquid cylinders. The contaminated gas is then vented from the propellant tanks through the thermal oxidizer to the atrnosphere.
Once the propellant is off-loaded, decontamination of the capsule occurs. Decontamination of the Dragon spacecraft will also occur in the Dragon Decontamination Building. It is estimated that up to eight times per year, Spacex will need to decontaminate a Dragon Capsule. During this operation, there is a small risk of propellant being released to the atnosphere due to the small amount of liquid and vapor monomethylhydrazine (MMH) and nitrogen tetroxide (NTO) that remain in the unit after use. SpaceX is proposing to vent the residual MMH and NTO to the existing thermal oxidizer during thg decontamination activities.

https://records.tceq.texas.gov/cs/idcplg?IdcService=TCEQ_EXTERNAL_SEARCH_GET_FILE&amp;dID=1393118&amp;Rendition=Web

https://records.tceq.texas.gov/cs/idcplg?IdcService=TCEQ_EXTERNAL_SEARCH_GET_FILE&amp;dID=1393128&amp;Rendition=Web</SimpleData>
				</SchemaData>
			</ExtendedData>
